- There was a variety, you know,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.700
we were talking to, you know,
it was everything from IMG

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.040
all the way to the, you
know, every sports team,

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.910
and a lot of it coming out
of college it's like also,

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.180
where do you want to move,
where do you want to live?

00:08:58.180 --> 00:08:59.013
- Yeah.

00:08:59.013 --> 00:09:00.840
- Going back to my SoCal story, like,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.916
I was freezing my ass off in Chicago.

00:09:02.916 --> 00:09:04.810
- (laughs) Done with this experiment.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.870
- I have to get back to California.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.190
And so one of the really
interesting ones that came up

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:12.230
was a sports marketing agency
that was in the Bay area

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.410
where it was called
Mel's Sport at the time,

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.390
and Visa International was their client,

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.140
and so it was basically to
lead Visa's Olympic games

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:21.840
and all the global sponsorships.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.130
So moved back to
California after undergrad,

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.003
lived in the Bay area.

00:09:26.003 --> 00:09:26.903
- What year is this?

00:09:26.903 --> 00:09:28.740
- This is 2000, class of 2000.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.820
- Yeah, so right from 2000.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.000
- Yes, right as the
dot-com, and before 9/11.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:34.860
- So you thought you were going

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.650
to the paved streets of gold.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:37.483
Right?
- A little bit, yeah.

00:09:37.483 --> 00:09:39.240
- I mean it was crazy then.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.073
- A little bit, it was a little crazy.

00:09:40.073 --> 00:09:41.910
- And then was it April
2000 when it melted

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.760
or it was April 2001, I
always try to remember.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.080
Do you remember, you graduated
in that class of 2000.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.233
Had the dot-com bubble
already, just bursted.

00:09:51.233 --> 00:09:53.553
- It was just after, 'cause
I had gotten a lot of,

00:09:53.553 --> 00:09:54.386
there was, you know.
- That's what I thought.

00:09:54.386 --> 00:09:55.268
That's what I thought, April 2000.

00:09:55.268 --> 00:09:56.940
- There was a lot of people
who were coming, you know,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:57.880
a lot of these dot-coms,

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.550
a lot of these people were
reaching out at the time,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.920
similarly, like every top.
- Like sportsportsports.com,

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:02.890
worth $40 trillion.

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:03.723
- Yeah, there's this, there's that,

00:10:03.723 --> 00:10:07.250
what was it, the pets.com
sock puppet on the Super Bowl,

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.603
right, all that kind of stuff.

00:10:08.603 --> 00:10:10.691
So literally--

00:10:10.691 --> 00:10:13.530
- So as you're kind of on
your way to graduation,

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:16.233
the world is unbelievably frothy.

00:10:16.233 --> 00:10:17.550
It's like right now.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.090
- Well it's like, yeah, I'm thinking like.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:20.548
- Easy.

00:10:20.548 --> 00:10:21.610
- We're gonna go get this thing,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.147
and also it was less...
- April 2000,

00:10:23.147 --> 00:10:24.560
dot-com bubble bursts.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:25.393
- Yeah.

00:10:25.393 --> 00:10:27.610
- You're going to the Bay,
so you go the Bay area

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.760
right as everyone's like, frowning.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:30.750
- Right, it's just kind of like, yeah,

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.083
what's going on out here?

00:10:33.024 --> 00:10:35.690
And yeah, so I started with Visa,

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:38.260
working for municipal,
but I worked in house,

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.410
in Visa's headquarters,

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:40.820
down in Foster City.
- Got it, got it.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:41.653
Make sense.
- And so,

00:10:41.653 --> 00:10:42.900
it was really cool, getting exposure,

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.470
so doing everything from working on,

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.920
you know, worked on the
Sydney, Salt Lake Olympics,

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:50.930
and the Athens Olympics
were the main ones,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.230
kind of, you know.
- Getting ready.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:54.580
- We did, and did everything from managing

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.810
a huge on site thing in Athens

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.880
with all the former Olympians,

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.610
called the Visa Olympian Reunion Center,

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:01.760
all the way to just, you know,

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.350
the comms and everything in between,

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:04.840
and just, it was a really great experience

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.390
because also, for that stage in my career,

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.450
being straight out of college,

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.670
the team there was very senior level.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.830
And getting exposure also,
at a global business,

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.110
'cause it was a global sponsorship.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.770
So we're talking like, Rugby World Cup,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:19.285
and things that were
going on in Australia,

00:11:19.285 --> 00:11:20.118
and all this stuff.

00:11:20.118 --> 00:11:21.800
It's a really great learning ground

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.050
to just understand--
- Were you a cliche American?

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.763
Being like, what the hell's rugby?

00:11:24.763 --> 00:11:25.596
Initially?
- Initially.

00:11:25.596 --> 00:11:26.740
And then I had to learn what a try is,

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.530
and do all the all blacks, the haka,

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:32.340
and all that, I learned
all that shit, man.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:33.680
It was a really cool experience

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.190
just kind of getting
exposure to that and so,

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:38.350
did that for a number of
years out at the Bay area,

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:41.950
and then straight after
the Athens 2004 Olympics,

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.250
came out east to business school,

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:44.970
which is where I went to Yale.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:46.520
And in between my first and second year

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:48.150
is where I discovered Pepsi, actually.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:53.150
Indra Nooyi, our former
CEO, was speaking on campus.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.230
And I was kind of the,
again, the dorky guy,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:57.360
went up to her afterwards and said,

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.560
hey, do you guys recruit?

00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.450
And I didn't think they did,

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.903
she's like, maybe we should, you know.

00:12:00.903 --> 00:12:02.101
- Let's talk about the dorky guy.

00:12:02.101 --> 00:12:03.270
- I'm a dorky guy.
- I know that,

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:04.570
that's why I'm jumping in on it.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:05.548
You said it.

00:12:05.548 --> 00:12:08.150
- That's true, it's true.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:09.690
- I view it a little bit different,

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.220
based on the stories, and
something I think a lot about.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:13.990
I said it yesterday, like, somewhere,

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:16.520
I don't remember, if you
don't ask, you don't get.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.730
You know, you send out these 300 things.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:20.412
You go up to Indra.

00:12:20.412 --> 00:12:21.920
- It's initiative.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:23.730
- Yeah, I'm also a person who can,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.680
like, who lives in a world
where I can see in people's eyes

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.520
they want to come up and do
something, and they don't.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.660
And because others do, I
can't get to that person,

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:34.240
and D-Rock's like, we're
gonna miss our flight,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:35.160
and we run away.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.110
And I think about those moments a lot.

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:39.260
I'm like, wow, that person
really wanted to ask something.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.630
They might have, 99% of the time,

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:43.730
they're not necessarily
saying something I need,

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:45.700
or am interested in, or you know.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:48.480
But, the amount, I mean, D-Rock emailed,

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.270
how many times did you, D-Rock
saw me speak at Columbia.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.103
Right?

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.050
Thought it was interesting,
and emailed me.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.750
- [D-Rock] Around eight
times, I think eight times.

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:58.810
- Eight times before I answered.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:01.737
Right, and I was, you know,
in the south of France.

00:13:01.737 --> 00:13:02.570
(all laughing)

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:03.790
- And here we are.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:04.623
- And so.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:07.190
- But you're right, there's something.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.140
- Yeah, no, listen, a lot of
times when I'm doing this,

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:10.510
like obviously I love
having you on the show,

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:11.420
but I'm desperately worried

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.790
about everybody listening
right now, and like.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.510
- Oh I know, 100%.
- This is such an example.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.220
And I just feel like people
need to, you need to act.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.270
Like, if you ask respectfully.

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:20.710
Like if you're the one, you know listen,

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:21.963
D-Rock emailed me eight times.

00:13:21.963 --> 00:13:23.430
There're people who like,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.250
inappropriately stand outside
my office and do other things.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:27.083
That's not gonna work

00:13:27.083 --> 00:13:28.470
if you're making the person uncomfortable,

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:29.380
if you're not bringing them value,

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.730
but if you respectfully ask,

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.310
you're putting yourself in a position.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:34.740
So Indra at Yale.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:38.560
You hear that and you
were impressed by her,

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.910
or Pepsi was so iconic,

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:42.640
or a mix of the both?

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.670
- One of the reasons I went
back to business school,

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:46.450
frankly, was when I was
in sports marketing,

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:49.090
I knew that there would be probably a cap

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:50.980
to kind of where I could
go, what I could do.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.518
- You got a taste of the landscape

00:13:52.518 --> 00:13:53.808
of the corporate world.
- I got a taste.

00:13:53.808 --> 00:13:55.440
- You said if I don't get an MBA,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:56.690
I'm not gonna be able to--

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.277
- Yeah, and it was less that, you know,

00:13:58.277 --> 00:14:01.060
and an MBA, the advice I
give whenever anybody asks me

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:02.460
about hey, should I go
back and get my MBA,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.380
should I do it?

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:05.400
I always say, listen.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.670
If you go to law school you're a lawyer.

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:08.010
If you go to med school you're a doctor.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.560
You got an MBA, you're a dude with an MBA.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:11.850
And so at the end of the day,

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.150
when you think about business school,

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.990
it's not about having the MBA

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.610
that actually does anything for you,

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:18.570
it's what you do with it,

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:19.980
it's how you broaden your horizons,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:21.770
it's the networking, it's
all the kind of stuff

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:23.920
that comes with it, and so that to me,

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.060
going back to get it, back to my story,

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.630
was about broadening my horizon

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.350
beyond kind of this world of sports,

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:30.670
and learning more about, you know,

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.432
the financial stuff, and marketing,

00:14:32.432 --> 00:14:33.265
and all these different elements.

00:14:33.265 --> 00:14:34.098
- Plus at a place like Yale,

00:14:34.098 --> 00:14:34.931
You're gonna bump--
- Organizational behavior.

00:14:34.931 --> 00:14:35.764
- Into all sorts of people.

00:14:35.764 --> 00:14:37.330
- Speakers and that's where, and so again,

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.260
when I was there it was less even about,

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:40.760
I'd go to class, I'd do all this stuff,

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.388
but I was there for the
whole experience and so,

00:14:43.388 --> 00:14:45.050
when you talk about going
to a place like Yale

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:46.220
where I was fortunate enough to go,

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:47.970
and the speakers, the people.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.280
- It's absurd.
- It was out of control,

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:50.580
and so going to all these things

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:53.300
and I would make the most
of just, to your point,

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:55.620
of just go to all the,
attend all these speakers,

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:56.910
go to all these things, I'd go,

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.680
I'd led a trip to Japan with
a group of my friends there,

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:01.890
where the people helped,

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.170
we connected with the head
of Nissan and all this stuff,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.325
I mean, like it's--

00:15:05.325 --> 00:15:06.290
- The access, the brand--

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.790
- It was a very cool--
- Plus Ivy League basketball

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:12.272
is always a fun, competitive
kind of like league as well.

00:15:12.272 --> 00:15:13.105
- Right, right.

00:15:13.105 --> 00:15:15.570
So that was really cool
there and ended up,

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:16.980
ended up interning for Pepsi

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.670
in between my first and second years.

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:22.690
And the irony was I had wanted
to go to Pepsi or big CPG

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:24.300
to kind of get beyond sports marketing

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:25.700
and just broaden my horizons.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:27.340
And guess where they had
placed me for my intern?

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:28.173
- Sports.
- Sports group.

00:15:28.173 --> 00:15:29.006
- Of course.
- So I worked for a guy

00:15:29.006 --> 00:15:32.220
named John Galloway, who
was an iconic at the time,

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:34.560
and was kind of one of my
mentors who brought me into Pepsi

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:36.730
and we, you know, got along like,

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.720
you know, two peas in the
pod and just made the most,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.223
and so I came back full-time.

00:15:41.685 --> 00:15:42.590
- You know what's funny about that story,

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:43.423
just for everybody.

00:15:43.423 --> 00:15:45.447
A lot of the things that
I tell my brother AJ,

00:15:45.447 --> 00:15:47.510
and I have a sports agency,
and a lot of the things

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:50.580
I tell kids before the draft is,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:52.300
you know, they're like, I
want to go second round,

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:53.710
I want to go third round, I'm like, bro,

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.490
I'd rather you go fifth
round than second round,

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.470
and have the person ahead
of you in the depth chart

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:02.410
be the kind of veteran who
actually wants you to succeed,

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:03.890
than you going second round,

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:05.690
and having a guy in the depth chart

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:07.750
who is worried about you,

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:10.160
and is undermining you, and
is teaching you nothing,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.060
or you have bad coaching, like, you know,

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.070
it's unbelievable how much that matters.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:14.903
- Yeah.

00:16:14.903 --> 00:16:15.736
No, and--

00:16:15.736 --> 00:16:16.569
- And that's what you
had in this internship.

00:16:16.569 --> 00:16:17.402
- It totally is.

00:16:17.402 --> 00:16:18.877
I had a great kind of mentor that,

00:16:18.877 --> 00:16:20.407
you know, definitely looked out for me,

00:16:20.407 --> 00:16:21.920
and also, I will tell you,

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:24.091
the intern program at
Pepsi is kind of funny,

00:16:24.091 --> 00:16:26.790
they do this thing every
year called the intern bash,

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:28.220
which, it was like kind of, you know,

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.050
they make the interns have a lot of fun

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:31.130
and do silly things.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:32.090
And I'm still convinced,

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:33.730
back to your idea of
taking the initiative,

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:35.970
you know, and I'm someone who
likes to put myself out there,

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:38.660
so when I was the intern, this year,

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.999
we had to do a 1980's fashion show,

00:16:40.999 --> 00:16:45.200
for the entire company,
outside in purchase, right?

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:46.190
And so everybody, you know,

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:47.640
someone's dressing up like Madonna,

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:50.820
someone's dressing up
like, you know, Miami Vice.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.360
I decided it's a good idea to do

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.000
Tom Cruise in Risky Business.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.180
Basically, tighty whities.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:57.780
White shirt, candlestick.
- You went there.

00:16:57.780 --> 00:16:59.000
You went there.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:00.000
- I went there.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.610
And quite literally I still
get asked by people about it.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:04.326
I'm convinced that's the
only reason I got hired.

00:17:04.326 --> 00:17:05.159
- I believe that.

00:17:05.159 --> 00:17:05.992
I believe that story.

00:17:05.992 --> 00:17:06.825
By the way, by the way.
- Literally.

00:17:06.825 --> 00:17:08.164
But that's the kind of thing,
just put yourself out there,

00:17:08.164 --> 00:17:09.078
it's like, just put yourself
out there, it's like,

00:17:09.078 --> 00:17:09.911
you know what--
- Todd, by the way.

00:17:09.911 --> 00:17:10.744
- I'm having a great time.

00:17:10.744 --> 00:17:13.060
- Literally, somebody that
worked at Vander for 10 years,

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.690
who's now my partner
