- I think the question is is
in a big corporate environment

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:52.530
like PepsiCo, to your point,

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:54.770
folks like me and you,
we're boom boom boom,

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:56.880
we're moving quick, we're
thinking, we're spitballing ideas,

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:57.853
throwing stuff out.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:01.170
Sometimes, and again,
it's a trained muscle.

00:36:01.170 --> 00:36:02.580
Sometimes you got to break a few eggs

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:03.920
to kind of make some omelets, right?

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:06.850
And get it going, and
so every now and then,

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.620
there's some times where
we're like, hey, go go go,

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:10.010
and then it's like, oh
well we can't do that,

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:12.140
'cause legal said this, oh we
can't do that because of this

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:14.590
and it's like, and I've
been trying to instill,

00:36:14.590 --> 00:36:15.740
hey, I don't need all those things

00:36:15.740 --> 00:36:18.870
to always boil up to me to
help kind of duke it out

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:20.820
with our, some cross functional teams

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:22.217
or figure out what we can and can't do.

00:36:22.217 --> 00:36:24.160
- Three for 10 is the hall of fame.

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:24.993
- That's exactly.

00:36:24.993 --> 00:36:26.360
300, right?

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:27.430
- Cal Ripken Jr.

00:36:27.430 --> 00:36:28.270
Eddie Murray.

00:36:28.270 --> 00:36:30.210
- Totally.
- These iconic Oriole players.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.777
(all laughing)

00:36:32.777 --> 00:36:33.693
- I love the Orioles in reference to like,

00:36:35.541 --> 00:36:36.374
I think that's good.

00:36:36.374 --> 00:36:38.300
- What do we want to end with?

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:39.695
For everybody who's listening.

00:36:39.695 --> 00:36:43.490
You have a slew of different
psychographics on my show.

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:46.100
You have a ton of young, aspiring but,

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:48.704
and an awful lot of people
more in our age group

00:36:48.704 --> 00:36:53.210
that I do think wish their
DNA was a little faster,

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.476
a little more spontaneous.

00:36:55.476 --> 00:36:57.650
Anything kind of last parting shot

00:36:57.650 --> 00:37:00.380
for people that are trying
to get into a happier place

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:01.730
or trying to accomplish more?

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:03.480
- Yeah and I think I wouldn't even

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:04.580
connect it to any of this stuff

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:06.500
on Pepsi or anything like
that but what I would say

00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:09.043
is kind of more what we
were talking about earlier.

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:11.990
Life is what you make of it,

00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:14.280
which I know is the most
cliche kind of thing out there,

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:16.280
but it's this idea, I
tell everybody at Pepsi,

00:37:16.280 --> 00:37:17.870
I mentor a lot of younger folks

00:37:17.870 --> 00:37:19.560
and people who are just coming in.

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:23.230
And a lot of people see the world

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:25.930
as it's presented to
them, not as it is, right?

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:27.220
So they'll get a job and say,

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:29.040
well my job description says, whatever,

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:32.220
and I'm like every job
I've been in at Pepsi,

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:34.480
I've kind of, not rewritten completely,

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:35.590
but you make it your own.

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:36.690
What is your take on it,

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:38.240
what are you gonna bring to this,

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:39.560
and what are you passionate about

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:43.430
as you connect the dots,
don't always ask and just say,

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.000
you know, here's the things
that you need to do here,

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:46.440
boom boom boom boom boom.

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:48.995
What can you do to kind
of make your job your own,

00:37:48.995 --> 00:37:49.894
and take in your own hands?

00:37:49.894 --> 00:37:51.360
- It's kind of like
brand marketing, right?

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:55.180
Every brand's, big brand's
sentence or mission statement

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:58.560
is so vague that how you
interpret going for it,

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.490
or doing it for one time,

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:03.010
or a life beyond the life,
or whatever it might be.

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:03.843
- Totally.

00:38:03.843 --> 00:38:04.676
- It's complete interpretation.

00:38:04.676 --> 00:38:05.509
- Well and it's, I mean,

00:38:05.509 --> 00:38:08.210
case in point, when I came
into the water business, right,

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:11.100
it was like, hey, we sell
Aquafina, we got our play,

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.600
we got the segmentation done by whatever.

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:15.170
And I was looking at it and I'm like,

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:16.840
it feels like, but I'm looking outside,

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:18.793
I'm seeing an explosion of premium water,

00:38:18.793 --> 00:38:20.611
I'm seeing all, and I'm like,

00:38:20.611 --> 00:38:22.550
this doesn't feel right, I'm like,

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:25.350
maybe we should take a step
back and rethink it and so,

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:26.790
I'm saying, if I'm leading water,

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:27.950
so let's turn it on its head,

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:28.900
let's come up with a new thing,

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.880
let's come up with some
new, and so it's like,

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:33.580
don't be afraid to push
or question the norms,

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:35.560
just because they're
presented to you, like,

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:38.140
see the world as it is, not
how it's presented to you.

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:39.730
- And deal with the ramifications.

00:38:39.730 --> 00:38:40.580
End with that, right?

00:38:40.580 --> 00:38:42.880
Which is like end, if you sucked,

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:47.010
and you innovated crappy
water, and you got fired, what?

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:48.880
You can always go do
something like honestly.

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:50.070
Like at some level.

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:51.510
Like one of the biggest ways

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:54.240
I think that I've been
able to help my friends

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:57.260
and employees like, do things,

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:00.010
is just push them very heavily on, what?

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:01.320
What will happen?

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:02.153
You'll get fired?

00:39:02.153 --> 00:39:02.986
Okay.

00:39:02.986 --> 00:39:04.290
Well that sucks, and that's bad.

00:39:04.290 --> 00:39:05.270
Do you have any savings?

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:06.890
You know and sometimes
the person's like, yes.

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:08.667
I'm like, you know, and
I'll really go into it,

00:39:08.667 --> 00:39:10.920
and I'm like, wait, you have
three years worth of savings.

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:11.753
- Totally.

00:39:11.753 --> 00:39:12.910
- And you're not trying
to push the envelope?

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:15.570
You can get fired and chill
for a year and still be okay.

00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:17.290
Other people are in debt and they can't.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:18.770
Maybe you can't push as hard.

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:21.320
But while you've decided to
push in your organization,

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:22.950
you're interviewing outwardly,

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.130
just in case you do get fired.

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:25.920
Like, what, what?

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:27.780
- Well it's this idea, and
it almost goes back to,

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:28.970
you know, when I was
playing tennis growing up,

00:39:28.970 --> 00:39:30.200
I was a competitive tennis player,

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:31.340
my dad used to always say,

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.840
'cause I would be a headcase sometimes.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:35.980
Play to win, don't play not to lose.

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:37.860
And I would get into this
defensive mode where--

00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:38.767
- That's what tennis is.

00:39:38.767 --> 00:39:39.600
I hate being

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:40.433
defensive in tennis.
- I would literally be

00:39:40.433 --> 00:39:41.450
the classic, I'm up five one,

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:42.670
and I would lose seven five.

00:39:42.670 --> 00:39:43.930
- Really?
- Because I would be like,

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:45.540
I wouldn't be thinking, I'd be having fun,

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:46.460
I'd be kicking A.

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:49.050
- Big shout out to Nate
Schroeder, on my team,

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:52.380
who's led me 5-2 three times,

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:54.950
and has lost 7-5 all three times.

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:57.570
- But that's the deal, it's kind of like,

00:39:57.570 --> 00:39:59.310
right when you start realizing like,

00:39:59.310 --> 00:40:00.807
shit, I'm killing this guy right now,

00:40:00.807 --> 00:40:02.490
and I'm going and I got this,

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:04.120
and then I get in my head and I'm like,

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:04.953
don't mess it up, don't mess it up,

00:40:04.953 --> 00:40:06.750
and then I change my
strat, I start lobbing.

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:08.108
- The whole team's
pushing me, we got to run.

00:40:08.108 --> 00:40:09.000
- That's it, sorry.
- But I got to tell you.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.060
No no, you don't be sorry.

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:10.940
- But that's totally the deal.

00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:13.030
- They need to be sorry
'cause I got to say this.

00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:15.350
There's something crazy about tennis.

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:16.730
Out of all, like, I...

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:19.120
Out of all the places on earth

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:21.110
that I've navigated in my life,

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:24.800
it's the only place where
I can get defensive.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:28.620
There's something crazy
about being up 5-1,

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:30.800
and losing that second game,

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:33.650
starting to like, visualize the comeback.

00:40:33.650 --> 00:40:35.330
- Totally.
- Then 5-3 comes.

00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:37.070
Now you're serving up 5-3.

00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:38.680
You should cruise into this.

00:40:38.680 --> 00:40:40.810
God forbid you lose
that game and it's 5-4.

00:40:40.810 --> 00:40:42.240
It's definitely now 5-5.

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:43.710
- The second it gets to 5-4,

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:44.830
you've lost.
- You understand this?

00:40:44.830 --> 00:40:45.663
- It's totally.

00:40:45.663 --> 00:40:47.010
- Like you're react, you--
- That's the deal.

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:48.800
- There's something about--
- It's so real.

00:40:48.800 --> 00:40:51.060
- Not even ping pong,
not even other places.

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:52.690
Something about that six...

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:54.880
- It's the mental slip,
and so play to win.

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:55.713
Not to lose.

00:40:55.713 --> 00:40:56.610
- Todd, thanks for being on the show.

00:40:56.610 --> 00:40:57.491
- Hey, thanks dude, appreciate it.

00:40:57.491 --> 00:40:58.450
- Thanks for everybody listening.

00:40:58.450 --> 00:40:59.283
We'll continue with this.

